{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"e7d9d0a5be64a006","name":"card_event_monsoon_rot","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/WorldAtWar/events/card_event_monsoon_rot","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"MONSOON ROT","text":"The defense of each enemy unit is set to 1.","flavorText":"The Burma Campaign (1941-1944) was very much affected by the geographical characteristics of the region and the tropical climate was particularly extreme during the 1940s. From May to November the heat was blistering and painful, and then the monsoon season came with extreme thunderstorms that soaked the soldiers and their equipment, followed by insects that attacked them ruthlessly and tropical diseases that spread like wildfire among the troops. This made logistics extremely difficult for both friends and foe, but this hit the Japanese harder than the Allies and by 1944 the Japanese forces suffered their greatest military defeat up to that point. In total the Japanese suffered 200,000 casualties and losses in Burma, and some claim that 78% of these losses were from non-combat causes such as starvation and disease.","faction":"Britain","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":6,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_monsoon_rot","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_monsoon_rot_title","SourceString":"MONSOON ROT","LocalizedString":"MONSOON ROT"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":6,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_monsoon_rot_text","SourceString":"The defense of each enemy unit is set to 1.","LocalizedString":"The defense of each enemy unit is set to 1."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_monsoon_rot_history","SourceString":"The Burma Campaign (1941-1944) was very much affected by the geographical characteristics of the region and the tropical climate was particularly extreme during the 1940s. From May to November the heat was blistering and painful, and then the monsoon season came with extreme thunderstorms that soaked the soldiers and their equipment, followed by insects that attacked them ruthlessly and tropical diseases that spread like wildfire among the troops. This made logistics extremely difficult for both friends and foe, but this hit the Japanese harder than the Allies and by 1944 the Japanese forces suffered their greatest military defeat up to that point. In total the Japanese suffered 200,000 casualties and losses in Burma, and some claim that 78% of these losses were from non-combat causes such as starvation and disease.","LocalizedString":"The Burma Campaign (1941-1944) was very much affected by the geographical characteristics of the region and the tropical climate was particularly extreme during the 1940s. From May to November the heat was blistering and painful, and then the monsoon season came with extreme thunderstorms that soaked the soldiers and their equipment, followed by insects that attacked them ruthlessly and tropical diseases that spread like wildfire among the troops. This made logistics extremely difficult for both friends and foe, but this hit the Japanese harder than the Allies and by 1944 the Japanese forces suffered their greatest military defeat up to that point. In total the Japanese suffered 200,000 casualties and losses in Burma, and some claim that 78% of these losses were from non-combat causes such as starvation and disease."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::WorldAtWar","isReserved":true,"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Britain/CandidateArtwork/t_butma_soldiers.t_butma_soldiers","SubPathString":""},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/MONSOON_ROT.MONSOON_ROT","SubPathString